# Delete and paste over ranges

Handle $ARGUMENTS. `check`, or no argument, runs the gate bound. `deep` runs `--blocks=3 --lines=3` and compares its failures with Findings. `lower` runs `--lower` after a fix removes a known finding. `case <identity>` runs `--case=<identity>` to print one case's full detail. `update` brings the verification in line with changed code, as Coverage record says.

**The property.** A user puts the caret anywhere, or selects any range, and presses Backspace, Delete or Return, types a letter, pastes, cuts and pastes back, bolds the range, or presses Cmd+B at the caret and types a letter. The text before the range and the text after it stay, in order, around what the command inserts, and the lines split and join only where the range and the fragment say. A paste at a caret, or over a range inside one text, whose last block has the caret line's type leaves the text after the paste in that line's block, which keeps its properties, as `EDIT-CLIPBOARD-MIDDLE-BLOCKS-401` says. Whole blocks pasted into an empty block whose type the first of them has land in that block, which keeps its key, as `EDIT-CLIPBOARD-EMPTY-LINE-001` says. A read-only inline changes only whole, as `EDIT-INLINE-READONLY-DELETE-001` in the Delete Commands section of `docs/editor-behavior/markdown-editing-spec.md` says. A required title keeps its slot. Backspace, typing or Return over whole blocks leaves the block the `EDIT-SEL-BS-001` note names in their place, and a delete leaves the same document whether its range comes as the target or through the selection. An element whose schema holds one text, such as Plate's code block, never gets a second text, as `EDIT-CB-RANGE-MARK-001` says. A block void's text is not editable content, so a typed letter lands in a text block, never in the void, and typing through the transaction's text insert, as plugins and DOM repair do, leaves the lines the typing command leaves. A command over a range replaces every letter in it, so a comment or caret anchored to a selected letter does not survive, even when the pasted text ends like the text it replaces. The result is the value loading it into a fresh editor gives, one undo gives back the document before, and a cut pasted back at the caret it leaves gives back the document. In a quote that holds text beside a paragraph, Return in a text line gives what it gives in that line wrapped in a paragraph, as rows M1 to M3 of `docs/plans/2026-11-23-mixed-content-lines-6.md` ask: the quote stays one quote, every letter keeps its properties, and the next letter lands at the start of the second half.

**The harm.** A delete or paste that drops, copies or moves text outside the selection destroys what the user did not select. Three open P1 bugs sit in these commands, t_31726070, t_b2ddef38 and t_ea3f0bf2. Eight fixes landed in the same commands between 2026-10-24 and 2026-11-02, each found one case at a time: efbc6d54d2, 02e4705837, 1b4aa31709, 7587a84c1e, fa33511739, 4fa60a34c7, e2116bf1b8 and 887caefa1b. Two more fixed a second text in a code block: 03cbb1bb61, where Cmd+B on part of its text threw, and 9b3552d0ca, where the letter typed after Cmd+B at a caret there was lost.

## What it checks

`packages/plitejs/scripts/delete-paste-ranges.ts` runs the real Plite editor commands on every case up to a bound. It checks the TypeScript code itself, not a model of it.

1. **Documents.** Every document of up to `--blocks` top-level blocks and `--lines` lines. Each block is a paragraph, a quote of one or two paragraphs, or a `code` block, whose schema content is `schema.content.text({ min: 1, max: 1 })` like Plate's code block. Each line is empty, two letters, or a letter, an inline of two letters and a letter, where the inline is a `link` or a read-only `guard`. A document holds at most one inline. The `titled` world puts a required `title` first, through `schema.content.prefix`, and neither the title nor a code block holds an inline. Letters run from `a` in document order, so each one names its place. Texts may carry a `bold` mark. Each document with a code block also comes once more with a second line in each code block, a line break and two letters, one past the line bound, because a code block keeps its lines in its one text. Each plain-world document without a code block also comes twice more with an `embed` first or last, a block void declared as `void: 'block'` like Plate's board, one past the block bound. The void holds no place and no line, so a range over every text block has only the void beside it. The `mixed` world holds eight documents of quotes that hold text lines beside paragraphs. A text line is a run of texts between two of the quote's blocks, or between a block and the quote's edge. The documents are `quote("ab" paragraph("cd"))`, the same quote with `b` bold, `quote(paragraph("ab") "cd")`, `quote("ab" paragraph("cd") "ef")`, that quote after a paragraph, that quote before a paragraph, two adjacent mixed quotes and `quote(paragraph("ab") "cd" paragraph("ef"))`. They hold no empty paragraph, link or guard, because the first baseline already lists range findings for a quote with an empty paragraph. A mixed identity names each text's marks and each element's properties with their values, as in `quote("a" "b"{bold} paragraph("cd"))`, where `{bold: false}` would name a mark set to false. So moving the mark to another text, or changing its value, changes the document's name. The script refuses two mixed documents with one name.
2. **Places.** Every offset of every text, an inline's own text included, so the caret before an inline and the caret at its inside start are two places. A range runs from each place to each later one.
3. **Commands.** At a caret: `editorCommands.delete` backward and forward by character, `insertBreak`, `insertText` of `X`, `replaceSlice` of four slices, and `toggleMark` of `bold` followed by `insertText` of `X` in a second update. Over a range: `deleteFragment` backward, the same `insertBreak`, `insertText` and `replaceSlice` of the first three slices, `addMark` of `bold`, and a cut, which reads `state.slice.get()` over what the delete removes, as the editable's cut does, runs `deleteFragment` and pastes the slice back with `replaceSlice`. When the root's default block takes the place of the whole blocks a range covers, the cut reads those blocks closed, so they paste back with their types, as `EDIT-SEL-CUT-ALL-001` says. The slices are `text`, one open paragraph `X`; `lines`, open paragraphs `X` and `Y`; `quote`, an open paragraph `X` and a quote whose open paragraph is `Y`; and `closedQuote`, a closed quote of paragraphs `X` and `Y`. `closedQuote` runs only at a caret in a quoted line or inside a link, the places the oracle gives its lines. In a paragraph line, at a caret or over a range inside one text outside an inline, `replaceSlice` also pastes two slices whose last block carries `checked: true`, a property the schema allows only on paragraphs. `checked` is open paragraphs `X` and `Y` that both carry it, which the fitter's local text candidate lands. `checkedQuote` is a quote of paragraphs `X` and `Y`, open on both sides, where only `Y` carries it. That is an editor copy of two quoted paragraphs, which the fitter's structural candidates land. `replaceSlice` also pastes two slices of blocks that the caret line cannot hold as they are. They run at a caret, or over a range inside one text outside an inline, when the line holds text before and after it and is not a code block. `quotes` is two quotes that each hold one paragraph, `X` and `Y`, open two levels on both sides, which is an editor copy across two quotes. `titles` is open titles `X` and `Y`. The oracle reads both as the `lines` slice. `quotes` skips a line that holds an inline, where it fails as the `quote` slice's listed cause does. Over a range that ends after a letter of its text, on lines without an inline, `replaceSlice` also pastes an open paragraph of `X` and that letter, named by its text, such as `paste Xb`. That paste ends like the text it replaces, which a fitter that matches by look reads as kept. At a caret in an empty line other than a title, `replaceSlice` also pastes `closed`, two closed blocks with the line block's type and properties that hold `X` and `Y`, as a Markdown or HTML paste gives. In a paragraph it also pastes `closedChecked`, the same blocks with `checked: true` added to the first, so the line takes a property it lacks. The oracle gives both the lines `X` and `Y`. One closed block of plain text takes the fitter's text candidate, which kept the line's block even before `EDIT-CLIPBOARD-EMPTY-LINE-001`, so the slice holds two. The title slot holds one title, so no copy pastes two into it. `deleteFragment` forward gave the same findings as backward at the gate, so ranges run only Backspace. Backspace over a range runs a second time on a fresh editor, with the range as `deleteFragment`'s `at`, because the browser deletes its input target range that way. Typing over a range runs a second time on a fresh editor, with the range as both the selection and `insertText`'s `at`, because the browser types over its input target range that way. It runs a third time through the transaction, `tx.text.insert` of `X` over the selection, which plugins and DOM repair call and which skips the command's whole-block replacement. Over a range that covers all of one line's text, as a triple-click selects it, the script also runs a triple: it selects the range and writes the `tripleClickedBlock` annotation with the line's block, as the click does, runs `deleteFragment` without a range in a second update, as a headless call right after the click does, and types `X` in a third update, as the browser does after a triple-click and Backspace. In the mixed world only Return runs, at every caret.
4. **Oracle.** The oracle is a line model of the document before. It holds each text block's text, in order, with the read-only inline's span. Each command gives the lines it may leave:
   - A range or a caret command replaces the text from its start to its end with what it inserts, and the text before the range ends the first inserted line and the text after it starts the last. Cmd+B and a letter at a caret insert `X`, like typing.
   - `EDIT-CLIPBOARD-MIDDLE-BLOCKS-401`: a closed quote pasted at a caret in a quoted paragraph lands its paragraphs in that quote, so its first line ends with the text before the caret and its last starts with the text after it, as the open `lines` slice does. A caret at a link's inside edge and the caret just outside the link are one place in the line. In a line outside a quote, the paragraph cannot hold the quote, so the quote lands whole between the line's two halves. No spec rule names that case. Both halves hold text there, because a link sits between two letters.
   - `EDIT-CLIPBOARD-MIDDLE-BLOCKS-401`: after either `checked` paste, the line that holds the text before the paste keeps the caret line's block. So does the line that holds the text after the paste, when there is any, as a split's tail does. A pasted line that holds no text of the caret line keeps its own block.
   - `EDIT-CB-RANGE-MARK-001`: bold over a range keeps every line's text. Where the range covers part of a code block's text, that text stays unmarked, and the rest of the range takes the mark.
   - Backspace at a line's start joins it into the line before, and Delete at a line's end joins the next line into it. At the first or last line nothing changes. Each line of a code block's text is a line of its own, so a join into or out of a code block moves only the line it reaches, as `EDIT-CB-JOIN-BEFORE-001` says.
   - A range from a text's start to the start of a later line may also end at the end of the line before, because `deleteText` unhangs such a range, unless the range ends at the end of the document. A void after the last line means the range does not end there. No spec rule names this case. The `EDIT-SEL-BS-001` note covers only a range over all of one block's text, which leaves that block empty.
   - `EDIT-INLINE-READONLY-DELETE-001`: a command with the caret or both edges inside the read-only inline, its edges included, changes nothing, and so does a delete with either edge inside it. Backspace or Delete from the text beside it removes it whole. Typing, Return or paste over a range with one edge inside it replaces the range with the inline included.
   - In the titled world, a command that joins every body line into the title may leave one empty body line, because the body keeps an occupant. Backspace or Delete at a caret may instead change nothing, as fa33511739 keeps an empty title only when no block must be built in place of the joined one.
   - `EDIT-ATOMIC-BS-AFTER-EMPTY-001`: Backspace in an empty paragraph right after the void removes that paragraph.
   - `EDIT-SEL-TRIPLE-BS-001`: after a triple, the letter starts the next line when the block after the selected one takes text, because Backspace removed the selected block. A required title, a line of a quote with two lines, the last block and a block before the void stay, empty, and take the letter.
   - Plite core has no quote rules. The spec's blockquote rules, such as `EDIT-BQ-ENTER-EMPTY-001`, belong to Plate's blockquote plugin, so a quote here is a plain container.
   - Rows M1 to M3 of `docs/plans/2026-11-23-mixed-content-lines-6.md`: the line model reads a quote that holds text beside a block as text lines and blocks, so each text line is a line of its own. Return in a text line gives what it gives in that line wrapped in a paragraph, and Return in a paragraph there splits the paragraph. For the mixed world the oracle also builds the whole document Return leaves: the line becomes two paragraphs in its container, each half keeps its letters with their properties, an empty half holds one empty text without marks, and every other node stays as it was. The letter typed next lands at the second paragraph's start, with the marks of the text that paragraph starts with, or of the text the caret sat in when that paragraph is empty.
5. **Laws.** After each command the script compares the editor with the oracle. A case records its first failure:
   - `range-read-only`: a read-only inline whose text changed but not whole, or a changed document where the oracle says nothing changes.
   - `range-slot`: a titled document whose first block is not a `title`.
   - `range-canonical`: a document that loading would change: an element with no children, two texts with the same marks side by side, an empty text no inline needs, or an inline without text on both sides. Inside an inline the same holds, so a bolded part of a link is a text of its own. An inline may hold no text after any command except typing or paste at a caret, because loading keeps an empty link and `EDIT-INLINE-BS-EMPTY-001` says how Backspace removes one. Typing or paste at a caret never leaves one. In the mixed world, the law reads each text line between an element's blocks as a line of its own, so an empty text at a text line's edge counts only as the spacer an inline element needs, as in `quote(paragraph("ab") "" link("cd") "" paragraph("ef"))`. In every other world, the law reads an element that holds text as one line, so a block inside it fails. In the mixed world, the law also loads the result into a fresh editor and fails when loading changes it or throws.
   - `range-text`: the text, read across all lines, differs from every form the oracle allows. The command lost, copied or reordered text.
   - `range-lines`: the text matches but the lines split or join elsewhere.
   - `range-props`: after either `checked` paste, a line's block, read as its type and properties, differs from the block the oracle gives it.
   - `range-line-key`: after the `closed` or `closedChecked` paste, the empty line's block no longer has its key, which `EDIT-CLIPBOARD-EMPTY-LINE-001` says it keeps. A refused paste is out of it.
   - `range-kept`: a command over a range other than bold changed the document but kept a selected letter. Before the command, the script puts an inward anchor that drops on deletion over each selected letter, and after it every anchor must resolve to nothing. The text and lines laws cannot see this, because a kept letter and a pasted copy of it read the same.
   - `range-undo`: one undo does not give back the document before.
   - `range-cut-paste`: the cut's slice, pasted back at the caret the cut leaves, does not give back the document. Ranges with an edge inside the read-only inline get no cut, because the spec says such a cut removes nothing.
   - `range-throws`: a command or its undo throws an error other than a refusal, or the editor refuses bold over a range. `EDIT-CB-RANGE-MARK-001` says the mark still applies outside a code block's partly covered text, so a refusal there loses the user's Cmd+B.
   - `range-void`: the void holds text after typing or any other command. The law also reads the transaction's typing.
   - `range-type-tx`: typing over a range through the transaction leaves other lines than the typing command.
   - `range-type-at`: typing over a range with the range as its target leaves other lines than typing over it through the selection. The law compares lines only. Compared as documents, the two paths differ in 138 gate cases on whether the letter joins a link the range ends at, and no spec rule picks one: `paragraph("") paragraph("a" link("bc") "d") :: type X over [0,0]:0..[1,0]:1` puts `X` before the link with the range as its target and inside it through the selection.
   - `range-block`: Backspace, typing or Return over whole top-level blocks leaves another block in their place than the `EDIT-SEL-BS-001` note says. A range from a block's start to the end of a later block, or to the start of the block after it, keeps its first block, emptied, with its type, when another block stays beside them, and otherwise leaves the root's default paragraph, as `EDIT-SEL-CUT-ALL-001` says. Typing and Return over such a range leave that block in the same place, with the letter in it or split at its start, as `EDIT-SEL-TYPE-BLOCKS-001` and `EDIT-SEL-ENTER-001` say. The law reads typing through the selection and with the range as its target. It reads typing only over several blocks or over a quote, because typing over one block that holds its own text, such as a paragraph or a code block, replaces the text in place. Typing and Return over a quote of two paragraphs, and Return over every block of the root, are out of it, because their landing waits on the open work of `docs/plans/2026-11-23-commands-next-step.md`. Backspace removes such a quote whole, and Return over the whole root keeps the raw break, as a delete over the root without a range does. The law reads only the type and properties of that block, and of a quote's first paragraph, because the lines law already reads the text. A range that starts at the title's start is out of it.
   - `range-at`: Backspace over a range with the range as its target leaves another document than Backspace over it through the selection. The key passes its range as the target, and a headless call often reads the selection, so the two must agree.
   - `range-tree`: a Return in the mixed world whose document differs from the one the oracle builds. The law compares the whole document, so a quote split in two, a line left outside a paragraph, or a letter whose properties changed in either half or in an untouched sibling fails it. It runs after `range-lines` and before `range-undo`.
   - `range-next-letter`: a letter typed right after a Return in the mixed world, on a separate replay, whose wrapper type, parent, rooted path, offset or marks differ from what the oracle gives. The law first checks that Return leaves a caret, not a range or a selection of nodes. After the letter, it compares the text of every line with the oracle's lines, with the letter at the start of the second half. So a Return that selects the next letter, which the typed letter then replaces, fails it. It runs after `range-undo`.
6. A refusal the editor gives to any other command counts in the summary's `refused`, and the laws then compare the document it kept.
7. It compares the findings with five baselines through `tooling/scripts/finding-baseline.mjs`: documents without a code block or a void with `packages/plitejs/scripts/delete-paste-ranges-baseline.json`, documents with a code block of one line with `packages/plitejs/scripts/delete-paste-ranges-code-baseline.json`, documents whose code blocks hold a second line with `packages/plitejs/scripts/delete-paste-ranges-code-lines-baseline.json`, documents with a void with `packages/plitejs/scripts/delete-paste-ranges-embed-baseline.json`, and the mixed world's documents with `packages/plitejs/scripts/delete-paste-ranges-mixed-baseline.json`. The code, code-lines and void documents have baselines of their own because most of their findings repeat the first baseline's causes under new identities, and a baseline never grows. The mixed world has its own so that each key step of `docs/plans/2026-11-23-mixed-content-lines-6.md` empties the findings its key fixes. Every finding is filed under `packages/plitejs/src/transforms-text`. An identity names the document, the command and its places as `[path]:offset`, never the result.

## Run it

Run every command from the repository root.

- `pnpm check:delete-paste-ranges` checks the gate bound of two blocks and two lines. It compares 23,125 cases on the 84 documents without a code block or a void, 8,643 cases on the 42 with a code block, 17,776 cases on the 42 whose code blocks hold a second line, 38,414 cases on the 136 with a void and 73 cases on the 8 mixed documents. The triple added 106, 49 and 172 of them, the two `checked` pastes add 1,580, 400, 400 and 2,760, `quotes` and `titles` add 368, 100, 100 and 608, `closed` adds 46, 31, 8 and 84, and `closedChecked` adds 46, 8, 8 and 84. Before the code block and bold, its 17,792 cases cost 209 s of processor time, about 12 ms a case, spread over 16 workers, so they needed about 13 s on a machine with 16 free cores. The 28,234 cases took 26 s at a load average of 42, and the 61,990 with the void documents took 24 s on 16 workers. At a load average of 110, the 62,687 cases with the `range-kept` law and the echo pastes took 137 s, and the 61,990 before them took 165 s right before, so the law's cost is within the host's noise. The 81,327 cases with the second code line took 223 s and 245 s on 16 workers while browser runs shared the host. The 83,115 cases with the `checked` paste took 184 s while other checks held most of the host's 18 cores, and the 86,540 with `checkedQuote` took 247 s at a load average near 60. The 87,716 with `quotes` and `titles` took 380 s and 333 s at a load average near 70. Most of a case is the command, because one `deleteFragment` on a two-paragraph editor costs about 5 ms. It prints one JSON summary line for each baseline, whose `code`, `mixed` and `documents` fields say which, then each finding that baseline does not list. `pnpm check delete-paste-ranges` runs the same step through the check runner, and Local CI runs it on next after each landing.
- `pnpm check:delete-paste-ranges --light` runs a fixed half of each gate's cases at the gate bound. A hash of the case's identity picks the half, so every light case is a gate case with the same identity. It compares 11,563, 4,319, 8,890 and 19,207 cases, half of each of the four gates, and 40 of the mixed world's 73, its witnesses included. Before the code-lines gate, its three gates took 35 s at a load average near 166, and with it one run through the check runner took 170 s. With the `checked` paste it took 83 s, and with `checkedQuote` 159 s at a load average near 60. With `quotes` and `titles` it took 337 s at a load average near 70. Each light run fails on a finding its baseline does not list and passes a listed finding it does not reach. It also fails when a light count misses its pin, the `lightCases` field of its `GATES` entry. It takes no flag but `--jobs`. A session runs `pnpm check delete-paste-ranges --light` before a diff panel and the cases its change targets, with `--case`, while it fixes. Local CI runs the gate after landing.
- `--blocks` and `--lines` raise the bound. Three blocks need three lines, so the gate holds one and two blocks, and `--blocks=3 --lines=3` is the deep run. A bound other than the gate's or `--case` skips the baseline and exits 1 on any failure.
- `--case='<identity or part of one>'` runs only the matching cases and prints each failure's full stack, such as `'--case=paragraph("ab") paragraph("cd") :: paste quote at [0,0]:1'`.
- `--jobs=<n>` sets the number of worker processes. It defaults to `CHECK_CORES`, the cores the check runner reserves for the step, and otherwise to the machine's cores up to 16.
- Exit 0 is a pass and exit 1 a finding. Exit 2 is a bad flag or a crashed worker. At the gate the run also fails when any baseline's documents give a case count other than its pin, the `cases` field of its `GATES` entry, now 23,125, 8,643, 17,776, 38,414 and 73. So a change that stops documents or places from generating cannot shrink the gate while the baselines still match. Update a count only with the reason it moved.
- The mixed world's `GATES` entry names witnesses, which run in every bound, the light one included: rows M1 and M3 of `docs/plans/2026-11-23-mixed-content-lines-6.md` and Return at `[0,0]:1` in `quote("ab" paragraph("cd") "ef")`, and at `[0,0]:1`, `[0,1]:0` and `[0,1]:1` in `quote("a" "b"{bold} paragraph("cd"))`, the mark's edge from each side and the bold text's end. The run prints every mixed case it runs with its first finding or `pass`, and fails when a witness did not run, unless `--case` narrows the run. A run where a witness did not run writes no baseline, under `--lower` or `--init` too.
- After a fix removes a known finding, the gate fails until you run `pnpm check:delete-paste-ranges --lower`, which lowers every baseline. A baseline never grows. `--init` seeds only a rule a baseline lacks. It fails on a finding that baseline does not list under a rule it lists, and checks the other baselines as usual. `--lower` and `--init` write nothing to a baseline whose case count misses its pin.

## Read a failure

A finding prints its rule, identity and the first line of its detail:

```text
range-lines paragraph("a" link("bc") "d") paragraph("") :: Backspace over [0,1,0]:0..[1,0]:0
  gives the lines ["","a"], not ["a"]

range-text paragraph("ab") paragraph("cd") :: paste quote at [0,0]:1
  gives the text "abcd", not "aXYbcd"
```

Both are known findings. The first deletes from the link's inside start to the start of the empty last line and leaves the empty line first, which is t_31726070. The second pastes a slice that ends in a quote between `a` and `b`, and `replaceSlice` returns false and pastes nothing. Run `--case` on the identity for the full detail.

Tell a real bug from a modeling error before you change code:

- **Real bug.** A user can make the selection and press the key, and the document loses, copies or moves text, or keeps a shape the oracle rules out. Fix the command, then run `--lower`.
- **Modeling error.** The oracle claims one result where the spec allows another, such as a structural rule of a block type this schema does not have. Fix the oracle in the script with the spec rule that allows it, and give the rule ID in its comment.
- **Intended change.** A spec change to what a command does to its lines changes the oracle too. Change the spec rule and the oracle together. Never weaken a law or add a baseline entry to get green.

## Findings

The first baseline lists 2,785 findings, each with one of sixteen causes. The counts by cause add up to 2,945, because later fixes lowered the baseline without lowering them:

- **A slice that ends in a quote.** Since the t_02275bcc fix, `replaceSlice` of `quote` inside a paragraph's text lands, so `paragraph("ab") paragraph("cd") :: paste quote at [0,0]:1` gives `["aX","Yb","cd"]`. At a line's end its first line does not join the line, so `paragraph("ab") paragraph("cd") :: paste quote at [0,0]:2` gives `["ab","X","Y","cd"]`, which is t_ea3f0bf2. At a line's start or inside a link, its open end does not join the text after the caret, so `paragraph("a" link("bc") "d") :: paste quote at [0,1,0]:1` gives `["abX","Y","cd"]`. 746 findings.
- **Typing, Return or paste writes into part of a read-only inline.** `paragraph("") paragraph("a" guard("bc") "d") :: paste lines over [1,0]:1..[1,1,0]:1` leaves the inline holding `c`. The spec's ownership note says these commands remove a selection through `removeText`, which skips the read-only test. 603 `range-read-only` findings.
- **Typing, Return or paste over a range with an edge in a read-only inline splits the lines.** `paragraph("") paragraph("a" guard("bc") "d") :: type X over [0,0]:0..[1,1,0]:1` gives `["X","d"]`, not `["Xd"]`. 631 findings.
- **A cut at a link's edge copies an empty link.** `paragraph("") paragraph("a" link("bc") "d") :: cut over [1,0]:0..[1,1,0]:0 and paste it back` gives `a`, an empty link and the link, so pasting it back splits the link. 532 `range-cut-paste` findings.
- **A join out of a quote whose paragraph holds an inline leaves the quote.** This is t_b2ddef38. `mergeNodes` in `merge-nodes.ts` now removes the quote a join empties whatever the joined paragraph holds, so `paragraph("") quote(paragraph("a" link("bc") "d")) :: Backspace at [1,0,0]:0` gives `["abcd"]`. That fix dropped 138 of the cause's 152 findings, every Backspace and Delete among them. 14 findings stay counted here.
- **A slice that ends in a quote, pasted at a link's inside start, splits the link.** `paragraph("a" link("bc") "d") :: paste quote at [0,1,0]:0` gives `a`, an empty link and `X`, then the quote and the line `bcd`. Every other slice lands just outside the link. This one does not land there, so the fit keeps the inside edge. 11 `range-canonical` findings.
- **A range from the title into the only body block does nothing.** `title("ab") paragraph("cd") :: Backspace over [0,0]:0..[1,0]:1` keeps `abcd`, and so do Return, typing and paste over the same range. 94 findings.
- **A cut of a range that hangs into the next line copies the line break it does not delete.** `paragraph("") paragraph("ab") :: cut over [0,0]:0..[1,0]:0 and paste it back` gives an extra empty line, because `deleteText` unhangs the range and the slice read does not. 88 findings.
- **A range out of or into a quote at a paragraph that holds an inline does nothing.** `quote(paragraph("a" link("bc") "d")) paragraph("") :: Backspace over [0,0,1,0]:0..[1,0]:0` keeps `abcd`, and so does `paragraph("") quote(paragraph("a" link("bc") "d")) :: paste text over [0,0]:0..[1,0,0]:1`. 66 findings.
- **A cut across a quote's or the title's edge does not paste back in place.** `paragraph("ab") quote(paragraph("cd")) :: cut over [0,0]:1..[1,0,0]:2 and paste it back` gives the paragraphs `a` and `b` before the quote, not `ab`, and `title("ab") paragraph("cd") :: cut over [0,0]:1..[1,0]:0 and paste it back` adds an empty line. 22 findings.
- **Return or paste over a range that ends at the start of the empty last line keeps that line.** `paragraph("") paragraph("") :: paste text over [0,0]:0..[1,0]:0` gives `["X",""]`, where Backspace over the same range leaves one line. 40 findings.
- **Paste over a range that ends inside a link in a quote does not join the lines.** `paragraph("ab") quote(paragraph("c" link("de") "f")) :: paste text over [0,0]:0..[1,0,1,0]:1` gives `["X","ef"]`. 20 findings.
- **A range from an inline's edge to the start of the empty last line puts the empty line first.** `paragraph("a" link("bc") "d") paragraph("") :: Backspace over [0,1,0]:0..[1,0]:0` gives `["","a"]`. This is t_31726070. 14 findings.
- **Typing through the transaction over a range that ends at the start of the empty last line keeps that line.** `quote(paragraph("ab") paragraph("")) :: type X over [0,0,0]:0..[0,1,0]:0` gives `["X",""]` through `tx.text.insert`, where the command's whole-block replacement gives `["X"]`. `deleteText` unhangs the range, as for Return and paste above. 8 `range-type-tx` findings.
- **Typing, Return or paste over a range that ends at the read-only inline's inside start keeps the letters before it.** `paragraph("") paragraph("a" guard("bc") "d") :: paste text over [0,0]:0..[1,1,0]:0` gives `["X","abcd"]` and keeps the selected `a`. The command unhangs the range as if offset 0 of the inline's text were the line's start, and the oracle allows that unhung form, so only the anchor on `a` shows it. `EDIT-INLINE-READONLY-DELETE-001` says such a range is replaced with the inline included. 66 `range-kept` findings.
- **Backspace through the selection over the whole document keeps an empty quote.** `quote(paragraph("ab")) paragraph("cd") :: Backspace over [0,0,0]:0..[1,0]:2` leaves `quote(paragraph(""))`, where `EDIT-SEL-CUT-ALL-001` leaves one empty paragraph. A delete without a range that empties the whole document takes the transform's whole-block removal, which keeps the first block's type, because history cannot yet redo the command's landing after a collaborator moves or joins the kept block's content. With the range as its target the delete takes that landing, which leaves the paragraph. 31 `range-block` findings. The history peer-undo plan's phase 2, `docs/plans/2026-11-12-history-peer-undo.md`, owns that redo; once its step 17 lands, the delete without a range takes the landing and `--lower` removes these findings.

Bold and the echo pastes give none of them. The code baseline lists 1,260 findings. The counts below add up to 1,359 for the same reason. Of the counts, 1,121 are a cause above, because the same document with a paragraph in place of the code block has a listed finding for the same command and places, and 29 are the whole-document cause above, which also keeps an empty code block, as `code("ab") paragraph("cd") :: Backspace over [0,0]:0..[1,0]:2` shows. The other 209 come from three causes of the code block:

- **A line that holds an inline never joins into a code block.** `code("ab") paragraph("c" link("de") "f") :: Backspace at [1,0]:0` keeps `["ab","cdef"]`, and a range from the code block into that line deletes its text but keeps two lines, as `code("ab") paragraph("c" link("de") "f") :: Backspace over [0,0]:2..[1,0]:1` gives `["ab","def"]`. The code block allows one text, so it cannot take the inline, and no spec rule says whether the join should drop the inline or do nothing. 90 findings.
- **A code block never joins into a quote's paragraph.** `quote(paragraph("ab")) code("cd") :: Backspace at [1,0]:0` keeps `["ab","cd"]`, where a paragraph in place of the code block joins. 76 findings.
- **A cut across a code block's edge pastes back with the other block's type.** `code("ab") paragraph("cd") :: cut over [0,0]:0..[1,0]:1 and paste it back` gives two paragraphs. 43 findings.

The code-lines baseline lists 3,446 findings. The counts below add up to 3,714 for the same reason. 30 are the whole-document cause above. Of the other 3,684, 1,248 are a code-baseline cause, because the same document with each code block cut to its first line has a listed finding for the same command and places. Another 64 come from the join `EDIT-CB-JOIN-BEFORE-001` asks for:

- **A cut into a later code block's first line pastes back as two code blocks.** `code("ab\ncd") code("ef\ngh") :: cut over [0,0]:1..[1,0]:1 and paste it back` gives `code("ab\ncd") code("ef") code("gh")`. The join moves only `f` out of the later code block, as slatejs.org and platejs.org do, and the paste cannot put `ef` back in front of `gh`. Before the plate-private#175 fix the join moved every code line into the block before, which pasted back in place but turned the code into that block's text. A cut from the first code block's start pastes back in place.

The other 2,372 are not sorted by cause yet. The plate-private#191 fix removed 56 cut findings in documents whose code block holds a second line. Most cut from one block's start to the next block's start, such as `paragraph("ab") code("\ncd") :: cut over [0,0]:0..[1,0]:0 and paste it back`, and now paste back in place. Some repeat the cause of a slice that ends in a quote, such as `paragraph("ab") code("cd\nef") :: paste quote over [0,0]:0..[0,0]:1`. Others paste paragraphs at a code block's start and leave its lines in a paragraph: `paragraph("ab") code("cd\nef") :: paste lines at [1,0]:0` gives the text `abXYcd\nef` with the line break inside a paragraph. The one-line twin has the same landing, which no law saw, because without a second line the text matches.

The void baseline lists 4,133 findings. The counts below add up to 4,437 for the same reason. Of the counts, 4,401 are a cause above, because the same document without the void has a listed finding for the same command and places. The other 36 come from three causes of the void:

- **Typing through the transaction over a range that ends at the start of the empty line before the void keeps that line.** `paragraph("ab") paragraph("") embed("") :: type X over [0,0]:0..[1,0]:0` gives `["X",""]` through `tx.text.insert`. This is the first baseline's unhang cause for typing through the transaction, which the void reaches because the line is no longer the end of the document. 20 `range-type-tx` findings.
- **Backspace in the empty only paragraph of a quote right after the void removes it.** `embed("") quote(paragraph("")) paragraph("ab") :: Backspace at [1,0,0]:0` gives `["ab"]`. `EDIT-ATOMIC-BS-AFTER-EMPTY-001` names a paragraph right after the atom, not one inside a quote, so the oracle keeps the line. 10 findings.
- **Return over a range from inside the read-only inline to the start of the empty line before the void drops that line.** `paragraph("a" guard("bc") "d") paragraph("") embed("") :: Return over [0,1,0]:1..[1,0]:0` gives `["a"]`. 6 findings.

The mixed baseline lists 37 findings, one for each Return in a text line, 13 `range-lines` and 24 `range-tree`, because Return there splits the quote instead of turning the line into a paragraph. `quote("ab" paragraph("cd") "ef") :: Return at [0,0]:2`, row M1, keeps the lines `["ab","cd","ef"]`, and `Return at [0,0]:0` and `Return at [0,2]:2`, row M3, split the quote in two. Every Return in a paragraph passes. Step 3c of `docs/plans/2026-11-23-mixed-content-lines-6.md` fixes the key and empties this baseline.

The deep run compares 206,836 cases on 588 documents and finds 41,909. It ran before the code block and bold. It took 703 s at a load average near 130. None is a `range-throws`, `range-undo` or `range-slot` failure. It prints only the 38,702 the gate baseline does not list. Sorted by shape, all but 612 of those fall under the gate's causes, and those 612 come from two causes that need a third line:

- **A range from an empty line's start to the end of a later line removes the empty line before it too.** `paragraph("") paragraph("") paragraph("ab") :: Backspace over [1,0]:0..[2,0]:2` gives `[""]`, not `["",""]`. 400 findings.
- **A command that empties a quote's first paragraph also removes an empty paragraph after it in the quote.** `paragraph("") quote(paragraph("") paragraph("")) :: Backspace at [1,0,0]:0` gives `[""]`, not `["",""]`, and Delete, Return and typing do the same. 212 findings.

It ran before the oracle let a titled caret join change nothing, so its counts include those cases.

## What a pass proves

Bugs planted on this tree showed where the gate holds:

- Run on `86be6c213f`, where `deleteFragment` without `at` took the transform's whole-block removal, the gate gives 90 `range-block` and 16 `range-at` findings. `quote(paragraph("ab")) paragraph("cd") :: Backspace over [0,0,0]:0..[1,0]:2` keeps an empty quote there. With the range as its target the delete gave none. A delete without a range now takes the landing when blocks stay beside the deleted ones, which removes the 16 `range-at` findings, and the 90 `range-block` findings stay as the first baseline's whole-document cause.

- Run on `f9404b833e`, before 4ee5ac2467 recorded a paste from what the fitter wrote and kept, the gate gives 14 `range-kept` findings, such as `quote(paragraph("") paragraph("ab")) :: paste Xb over [0,0,0]:0..[0,1,0]:2`, which keeps the selected `b`. At 4ee5ac2467 it passes. Every one is a range into, out of or inside a quote. A paste over one text block shows none there. An echo with a bold `X` found the same 14, and a two-line echo found none.

- A mutant where `deleteText` in `packages/plitejs/src/transforms-text/delete-text.ts` no longer asks `hasEdgeInReadOnlyInline` gives 683 new `range-read-only` findings, such as `paragraph("") paragraph("a" guard("bc") "d") :: Backspace at [1,1,0]:0` deleting `b`.
- A mutant where `mergeNodes` in `packages/plitejs/src/transforms-node/merge-nodes.ts` never removes the ancestor a join empties gives 394 new `range-lines` and 37 new `range-cut-paste` findings, such as `paragraph("ab") quote(paragraph("cd")) :: Backspace at [1,0,0]:0` leaving `["abcd",""]`.
- Reverting 03cbb1bb61 in `packages/plitejs/src/transforms-node/set-nodes.ts` gives 129 new `range-throws` findings, such as `code("") code("ab") :: bold over [1,0]:1..[1,0]:2` refused with `Editor element "code" at [1] allows at most 1 children.`
- Reverting 9b3552d0ca in `packages/plitejs/src/transforms-text/insert-text.ts` gives 69 new `range-text` findings, such as `code("") code("ab") :: bold then type X at [1,0]:1` giving the text `ab`, not `aXb`.
- Typing at the range's own start anchor, not at the point the removal collapses to, in `packages/plitejs/src/transforms-text/insert-text.ts` gives 892 `range-type-at` findings in `--blocks=3 --lines=3 '--case=:: type X over'` and none at the gate, whose two lines leave no line before a start block the range drops. `paragraph("ab") paragraph("cd") paragraph("ef") :: type X over [1,0]:0..[2,0]:1` gives the lines `["abX","f"]`, not `["ab","Xf"]`, because that anchor falls back to the end of the line before.

- Letting a write take a whole-block removal's landing in a void, as `prepareAt` in `packages/plitejs/src/transforms-text/delete-text.ts` did outside a grid, gives 80 `range-void` findings at the gate, such as `paragraph("ab") paragraph("cd") embed("") :: type X over [0,0]:0..[1,0]:2` leaving only the void, holding `X`, and 80 `range-lines` findings for Return over the same ranges, which leave no line.
- Running the `closedQuote` slice on the slice fitter from before t_fc9f1701's fix, in `packages/plitejs/src/core/slice-fit/compiled-slice-fitter.ts`, gives 636 findings at the gate, 588 `range-lines` and 48 `range-text`. `quote(paragraph("ab") paragraph("cd")) :: paste closedQuote at [0,0,0]:1` gives the text `"aXYcd"`, not `"aXYbcd"`, and `quote(paragraph("ab") paragraph("cd")) :: paste closedQuote at [0,1,0]:2` gives the lines `["ab","cdX","Y",""]`, not `["ab","cdX","Y"]`.
- Running the `closedQuote` slice at carets inside a link on the slice fitter from before t_b960b839's fix gives 201 `range-lines` findings at the gate: 135 in a quoted line and 66 at a link's inside edge in a line outside a quote. `quote(paragraph("a" link("bc") "d")) :: paste closedQuote at [0,0,1,0]:1` gives `["ab","X","Y","cd"]`, not `["abX","Ycd"]`, because the quote splits in three. `paragraph("a" link("bc") "d") :: paste closedQuote at [0,1,0]:0` gives `["aXYbcd"]`, not `["a","X","Y","bcd"]`.
- Running the `checked` paste on the slice fitter from before the plate-private#191 fix, in `packages/plitejs/src/core/slice-fit/compiled-slice-fitter.ts`, gives 1,066 `range-props` findings at the gate: 326, 88, 88 and 564 in the four gates. `paragraph("ab") paragraph("cd") :: paste checked at [0,0]:1` puts the line `Yb` in the pasted block, with `checked: true`, instead of the caret line's plain paragraph.
